  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <div class="site-header-inner">
        <a class="logo" href="/">All Things Central Oregon</a>
        <nav class="nav-links">
          <a href="/bend/">Bend</a>
          <a href="/redmond/">Redmond</a>
          <a href="/sisters/">Sisters</a>
          <a href="/sunriver/">Sunriver</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-inner">
        <div class="kicker">Bend, Oregon</div>
        <h1>Bend Local Guide</h1>
        <p>
          Outdoor access, high desert weather, mountain views, river days, coffee shops,
          breweries, trailheads, and year-round Central Oregon living.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <main class="container">
      <section class="intro-card">
        <p>
          Bend is the place most people picture when they think of Central Oregon.
          You have the Deschutes River running through town, the Cascades to the west,
          dry high desert days, cold mornings, busy trailheads, and a food and brewery
          scene that keeps growing every year.
        </p>

        <p>
          This page is the main Bend hub for All Things Central Oregon. Over time, it
          will connect to deeper local guides covering restaurants, breweries, coffee
          shops, hikes, camping, fishing, neighborhoods, family activities, seasonal
          events, road trips, and more.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>What Bend Is Known For</h2>
        <p>
          Bend is known for outdoor recreation first. People come here to ski Bachelor,
          float the Deschutes, mountain bike Phil’s Trail, paddle alpine lakes, hike
          through lava fields, and spend as much time outside as possible.
        </p>

        <div class="grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Outdoor Access</h3>
            <p>Trails, lakes, rivers, lava lands, forests, and mountain routes are all close by.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>Food & Drink</h3>
            <p>Breweries, coffee shops, food carts, brunch spots, patios, and casual local favorites.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>Four-Season Living</h3>
            <p>Ski days in winter, river floats in summer, and quieter trail seasons in spring and fall.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Things To Do in Bend</h2>
        <p>
          There is no shortage of things to do in Bend. Summer usually means floating
          the river through town, escaping to the Cascade Lakes, spending long evenings
          outside, and dealing with more traffic than locals would prefer.
        </p>

        <p>
          Winter revolves heavily around Mt. Bachelor. Spring and fall are underrated,
          with cooler trail weather, fewer crowds, and some of the best conditions for
          hiking, running, biking, and exploring town.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Local tip:</strong> Bend can feel very different depending on the season.
          A July weekend, a smoky August afternoon, a snowy February morning, and a quiet
          October weekday are almost four different versions of the same town.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Popular Bend Guide Topics</h2>
        <p>
          These are the main Bend guides this site will build out over time.
        </p>

        <div class="guide-links">
          <a class="guide-link" href="/bend/restaurants/">Best Restaurants in Bend</a>
          <a class="guide-link" href="/bend/hiking/">Best Hikes Near Bend</a>
          <a class="guide-link" href="/bend/coffee/">Best Coffee Shops in Bend</a>
          <a class="guide-link" href="/bend/breweries/">Best Breweries in Bend</a>
          <a class="guide-link" href="/bend/things-to-do/">Things To Do in Bend</a>
          <a class="guide-link" href="/bend/camping/">Camping Near Bend</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Neighborhoods Around Bend</h2>

        <div class="grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Downtown Bend</h3>
            <p>
              Restaurants, bars, coffee shops, Drake Park, Mirror Pond, and one of the
              most walkable areas for visitors.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>Westside Bend</h3>
            <p>
              Close to trail systems, breweries, coffee, mountain access, and a lot of
              Bend’s outdoor culture.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>East Bend</h3>
            <p>
              Growing quickly with newer neighborhoods, parks, shopping, and easier
              access toward Redmond.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Food, Coffee, and Breweries</h2>
        <p>
          Bend’s brewery reputation is real, but the food scene has grown well beyond
          beer. You’ll find food cart pods, breakfast burritos, bakeries, brunch spots,
          casual patios, cocktail bars, pizza, sushi, and locally roasted coffee.
        </p>

        <p>
          Locals tend to have strong opinions on the best brewery patio, the best coffee
          shop for getting work done, which food cart lot is worth visiting, and where
          to avoid peak summer crowds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Outdoor Recreation Near Bend</h2>
        <p>
          Bend’s biggest advantage is how quickly you can reach completely different
          landscapes. Within a short drive, you can get to Mt. Bachelor, Tumalo Falls,
          Smith Rock, the Cascade Lakes, Newberry National Volcanic Monument, the
          Metolius River, and high desert trails east of town.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Living in Bend</h2>
        <p>
          Bend has grown quickly and continues attracting remote workers, outdoor
          enthusiasts, families, retirees, and people looking for a different pace than
          larger West Coast cities.
        </p>

        <p>
          That growth comes with tradeoffs. Housing is expensive, summer traffic can be
          frustrating, and wildfire smoke is part of the reality of living in Central
          Oregon. Still, the combination of scenery, climate, outdoor access, and local
          energy is why so many people stay.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="site-footer-inner">
        All Things Central Oregon is a local guide to Bend, Redmond, Sisters, Sunriver,
        La Pine, Prineville, and the surrounding high desert.
      </div>
    </footer>
